A||||4||Mach10||Exhaust idea: Guru opinion(s)? --->||||3||
Z||000000||Mach10||03-31-2001||11:18 PM||||Well, after shopping around for exhaust headers that would a)fit into the engine compartment, and b)bolt up to the (not quite  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) stock exhaust system, I have decided that all of the commonly available ones are far too expensive for my taste.<BR>Sooooo, I had this little brainstorm:<P>Say some fool was to take a stock system, and have the flanges sandblasted and cleaned.<P>Now, this same fool would bolt them up to a junk head for stability.<P>Now, he'd hack them off at the joints with a saw. He'd then ship them off to a friend who owns a foundry/smithy, and have him manufacture a new collector pipe that is as long as the stock one, but considerably wider, with a gentle narrowing down to the stock downpipe flange, which had also been chopped, or else a modified crossover pipe that would accept a larger input, and "funnel" down to stock size.<BR>This won't cost as much money as it would effort. Is my reasoning sound that a larger collector, while not being as efficient at a set of headers, would allow the exhaust to flow better? I don't feel like spending $1000Cdn on a set of steel tubes, thank you very much :P<P>Thanks!<BR>Sean<P>||||reg||3
Z||000001||Phaeton||04-01-2001||02:50 AM||||Power from exhaust comes two ways, tuned pulses determined by length and cross section and inertia deterimined by gas velocity.  <BR>Different length primary tubes will destroy any tuned pulses and going to a collector that has a cross section area larger than the sum of the primary pipes is going to slow down the flow to where the scavenge effect is non existant.<BR>Bigger is better does work after the muffler.||||reg||12
Z||000002||Mach10||04-01-2001||11:44 PM||||You could have just said "no"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Ok, fine. One more thing, then: Where the H@#L am I going to find headers for less than $1000CDN?!? The prices that I have seen are BEYOND ridiculous. Are there any more "normal" headers from other cars that will bolt to the stock system with a little modification? I shouldn't have to pay more than about 600-800CDN for these bloody things.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000003||fyerstarter||04-02-2001||01:06 AM||||I have no idea what a CDN is but there are several sets of Fiero headers available from West Coast Fiero for between $400-$600 US Dollars.   <A HREF="http://www.cmwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cmwestco.com</A> <p>[This message has been edited by fyerstarter (edited 04-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||WKDFIRO||04-02-2001||01:39 AM||||I know that I will be corrected if I am incorrect about this. I have only my observations being around the industry of Fieros and the people who create the aftermarket parts for them. <P>The "bloody" headers are going to be expensive. <P>Accept it.<P>With most of the headers that are out there you are paying for 3 completely different exhaust pieces. There is no magic machine that you can just put a bunch of pipes in and get headers out the other end. They all have to be assembled by hand. One section of one collector of one header at a time. Sure if you had corporation (take your pick of import aftermarket brands) and were selling them by the hundreds to all of the Hondas out there you make quantity and bring the price down. But that is not the case here.<P>Good and Bad - Fieros are not Hondas. They don't look like Hondas, they don't sell like Hondas and they sure as hell don't have the gargantuan resources that the Hondas (and even the classic "muscle cars") do for an aftermarket. So we Fiero owners have to deal with the fact that aftermarket parts for our cars are going to be expensive. Why? Because there aren't that many people in the world making them. Its among the high prices we pay to have for a unique, very misunderstood car. <P>Personally it makes me pissed off to go to a SEMA show and have them snicker at me for mentioning the "F" word, so I quit asking a long time ago.<P>If you wish to have more performance from the exhaust angle of things (unless you have already done this) you can port out the manifolds and go to 2 1/2 inch exhaust from the cat back (if you want to play safe in California) through a Flowmaster (or muffler of your choice.)Many members of our club have done this and have been very happy with the results. I went a slightly different approach myself and found out the hard way.<P>I would also suggest that you make a search (unless you have done that too)about the power enhancements of these headers. I beleive that it is still in debate about the efficiency of headers on a Fiero. Talk to people who have tried things with their Fiero and find out what has worked for them and what hasn't.<P>For us people "South of the Border" I beleive that you are refering to Canadian Dollars, correct?<P>None of us should have to pay more than we do for these things. But some of us do. <P>Those who don't, have Hondas.||||reg||9
